lua.h, luaconf.h, lualib.h and lauxlib.h are the release's files, unmodified.

lua-amalgam.c is generated from the release's src/ and holds its sources unmodified, in the
order upstream's onelua.c includes them: lzio lctype lopcodes lmem lundump ldump lstate lgc llex
lcode lparser ldebug lfunc lobject ltm lstring ltable ldo lvm lapi lauxlib lbaselib lcorolib
lmathlib lstrlib ltablib lutf8lib. Each internal header (lprefix.h, llimits.h, lobject.h, ...)
is inlined where first included; the four public headers stay #includes. Before the sources it
defines LUA_CORE, LUA_LIB, ltable_c and lvm_c, includes luaconf.h and makes the internal symbols
static (LUAI_FUNC, LUAI_DDEF, LUAI_DDEC), as onelua.c does, and after the C library's headers it sets
hidden visibility, so the Lua API stays inside the program. liolib, loslib, loadlib, ldblib, linit, lua.c and
luac.c are left out: scripts get no files, clock, process or dynamic libraries.

To regenerate: python3 amalgam.py <lua-5.4.8>/src lua-amalgam.c

adm_lua.c and amalgam.py in this folder are not part of Lua.
